**THE TREATY FOR THE 1% REALLOCATION OF MILITARY EXPENDITURES TOWARDS BIOMEDICAL RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT FOR THE PREVENTION OF HUMAN SUFFERING AND DEATH**

*(Short title: the treaty where you buy 1% fewer bombs and, in exchange, stop dying of things)*

WHEREAS, humanity spends [$2.72 trillion](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/economics/1-pct-treaty-impact.html) a year on methods of killing itself, which is enough to buy [850](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/appendix/extinction-surplus.html) bullets for every human alive every year, which is more bullets than any human has a reasonable use for;

WHEREAS, since 1900 the governments of Earth have spent [$170 trillion](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/problem/cost-of-war.html) on killing approximately [310 million](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/problem/cost-of-war.html) humans;

WHEREAS, the nations of Earth have been hitting each other for roughly 10,000 years because the other one hit them last, which is the conflict resolution strategy of four-year-olds, except four-year-olds eventually get tired and take a nap, and the undersigned nations invented naps and declined to apply them to foreign policy;

WHEREAS, these murdered humans include 930,000 physicians, 310,000 scientists, 620,000 engineers, 1.24 million nurses, 3.1 million teachers, and [102 million](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/problem/cost-of-war.html) children who will never grow up to replace them;

WHEREAS, diseases kill more people than all wars combined and, unlike wars, do not even have the decency to be quick about it;

WHEREAS, your chance of dying in a terrorist attack is approximately 1 in [30 million](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/solution/1-percent-treaty.html), and your chance of dying of a disease is 100%, and your current budget does not reflect this;

WHEREAS, every signatory to this Treaty will personally be dead within 80 years, which is mentioned not to be rude but because you seem weirdly calm about this;

WHEREAS, the governments of Earth possess nuclear weapons sufficient to end civilization [20](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/appendix/extinction-surplus.html) times over but have not cured Alzheimer's once;

WHEREAS, governments spend [604](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/strategy/earth-optimization-prize.html) dollars on the capacity for orphan manufacturing for every one dollar spent on the trials that might cure what is actually going to kill their citizens;

WHEREAS, the United States Department of Defense has failed seven consecutive audits and misplaced $2.46 trillion, which is to say $2.46 trillion just wandered off, like a cat, except that unlike a cat nobody put up posters, meaning the 1% this treaty requests is smaller than the rounding error on the money the Pentagon has already lost track of;

WHEREAS, without the cumulative losses from war and disease the average human would earn [$333,636](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/problem/cost-of-war.html) a year instead of [$14,375](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/appendix/political-dysfunction-tax.html). Every citizen pays twice. Once in taxes that built the weapons. Again, every year, in wages they do not earn, because the scientists and engineers and builders who would have made the world richer were killed before they could;

WHEREAS, global military spending has been growing [2.76%](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/solution/1-percent-treaty.html) a year, in real terms, for twenty years. If it keeps growing, every human alive today will owe about [$402,488](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/solution/1-percent-treaty.html) in taxes over the rest of their life to pay for it. Most of the money will buy explosions in countries they cannot find on a map;

WHEREAS, cutting one percent now stops that growth. This saves the average person about [$290,052](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/solution/1-percent-treaty.html) over their lifetime.

WHEREAS, the United States spent [$29 billion](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/problem/unrepresentative-democracy.html) a year on its military in 1939, in today's dollars. It won World War II. Then it cut military spending by [87.6%](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/economics/peace-dividend.html) in two years and produced the biggest economic boom in history. Today it spends [30.6](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/proof.html) times the 1939 figure, in peacetime. Therefor, unless the human genome has significantly degraded in the two generations since, a one percent reallocation should be manageable;

WHEREAS, since 1962 the regulatory delay between proving a drug is safe and letting dying humans take it has killed approximately [102 million](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/appendix/invisible-graveyard.html) people, waiting for a committee to decide whether the safe drug worked well enough;

WHEREAS, pragmatic clinical trials built into ordinary healthcare cost [$929](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/economics/1-pct-treaty-impact.html) per patient, instead of the usual [$41,000](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/economics/1-pct-treaty-impact.html). That makes trials [44.1x](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/economics/1-pct-treaty-impact.html) cheaper. It lets [12.3x](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/economics/1-pct-treaty-impact.html) times as many people join. The wait to test treatments for every known disease drops from [443 years](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/economics/1-pct-treaty-impact.html) to [36 years](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/economics/1-pct-treaty-impact.html);

WHEREAS, faster trials plus no more waiting for efficacy approval means the average treatment reaches patients [212](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/economics/1-pct-treaty-impact.html) years sooner than today;

WHEREAS, redirecting one percent is projected to prevent approximately [10.7 billion deaths](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/economics/1-pct-treaty-impact.html) and [1.93 quadrillion hours](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/appendix/dfda-impact-paper.html) of human suffering, which are not metaphors and refer to specific future humans with specific future plans for next Tuesday;

WHEREAS, someone you love is, at this moment, suffering from a disease;

WHEREAS, the treatment that would help them exists as an untested compound on a shelf, because the money that would have tested it was busy turning into a missile;

WHEREAS, that missile incinerated a child who would have grown up to discover the cure;

WHEREAS, you lose the treatment, you lose the scientist, you get the inflation, you get the tax bill, you get to pay for her murder;

WHEREAS, it would be nice if governments could arrange to murder 1% fewer of those children;

NOW, THEREFORE, the undersigned nations agree to try a different approach, as follows:

**Article I**: Each signatory shall redirect exactly 1% of its annual military budget to **the 1% Treaty Fund**, split as follows: [80%](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/strategy/earth-optimization-protocol-v1.html) to pragmatic clinical trials; [10%](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/strategy/earth-optimization-protocol-v1.html) to perpetual returns on [Incentive Alignment Bonds](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/solution/incentive-alignment-bonds.html); and [10%](https://manual.WarOnDisease.org/knowledge/strategy/earth-optimization-protocol-v1.html) to a Political Incentive Fund that supports campaigns of legislators who vote to implement and expand this Treaty, structured in each signatory nation according to its domestic campaign finance and public-official-compensation laws.

**Article II**: Transfers shall be automatic, immediate, and irrevocable. The money moves on the first of every month, the way your mortgage does, except nobody has to call and yell at anyone.

**Article III**: The percentage can go up. It never goes down. When the treaty works, a mandatory review raises it. Article I is written in percentages, not dollars, so when the treaty grows, the bondholder payouts grow and the Political Incentive Fund grows. The people being paid will want the treaty to keep growing. They will keep wanting this until war and disease stop killing people. Then they can stop.

**Article IV**: Compliance shall be verified by public ledger and independent audits. Relabeling a submarine as "humanitarian infrastructure" will be noticed, because submarines are large and loud and do not fit in the humanitarian infrastructure drawer.

**Article V**: Non-compliant parties will be given a stern talking to.

**Article VI**: Citizens can sue their own government in their own courts if it stops paying.

**Article VII**: Withdrawal requires unanimous consent of all parties plus 10-year notice. Your country would need every other signatory to agree, which requires a decade of phone calls none of them are going to return.

**Article VIII**: This treaty supersedes all conflicting domestic law. Including the subsection your legislature added at 2 a.m. last session specifically to make sure this couldn't happen.

**Article IX**: Entry into force upon signature by two states. Two signatures. That's the bar. Your species once got 193 countries to agree that chemical weapons were bad. This asks for 2.

IN WITNESS WHEREOF, the undersigned, being of sound mind (debatable) and tired of watching their loved ones die of preventable diseases, have executed this Treaty on behalf of populations who would have executed it much sooner if anyone had asked them, and on behalf of someone each signatory can picture right now who is currently dying of a disease that the money in Article I will help test a cure for.

Signed this day, ____________, in the year of our ongoing confusion.

---

[Nation Name]
"We choose life, I guess"

---

*P.S. - Yes, this includes space weapons. Nice try.*

*P.P.S. - "Cyber weapons" too. We saw you thinking it.*
